{
  "heading": "Step 5: Test the service and the controller",
  "axis": "language",
  "body": "A test unit is a class in `client/tests` or `server/tests` whose name ends with `Test`. Each method whose name starts with `test` is one test case. `Test(HelloTestSetup)` names the Setup that the test uses. The Setup file must be in the same folder as the test.\n\n`this.construct(CounterService)` makes the service in the same way that Tachyon makes it for the page. `Fake` marks the field `greetingClient`, and `construct` gives the fake to the service in place of the real `GreetingClient`. So the test does not call the server. A controller is made in the same way: `this.construct(GreetingController)` makes the controller, and the test calls `getGreeting` directly, without HTTP. `GreetingClient` needs no test, because Tachyon writes its method. The Ruby controller test also uses `construct`. In compiled languages whose step 4 tabs are still pending, the test makes the class itself, such as `new CounterService()` in Java or `GreetingController()` in Python, because the class needs nothing from Tachyon. The Tac languages that skipped step 4 have no `greetingClient` yet, so their test checks only `increment`. Lua has no Tac tab here, because the build refuses Fake in Lua Tac code with TY1308. Dart uses `construct<CounterService>(CounterService)` with a typed `Fake` field. Its generated `GreetingClient` is bound to this test’s BehaviorContext, and HelloTestSetup supplies a fake HTTP adapter returning the typed greeting data. The awaited `askServer` case exercises the service and its generated client decoder without a network request. Each service keeps the fake passed at construction; changing the test field does not replace an existing service’s dependency.\n\nC# uses `construct<CounterService>()` with a typed `Fake` field. Each test creates its own BehaviorSignal and BehaviorContext, then passes that context to the generated `GreetingClient` constructor. `HelloTestSetup` explicitly constructs the stateless `GreetingFakeHelper` adapter and returns the typed greeting data through the existing adapter boundary. The service captures its client when constructed. The three cases check increment, typed message decoding, and independent captured contexts: disposing the first context refuses its later call with `CANCELLED`, while the second service still succeeds. Changing or clearing the Fake field does not replace either existing service’s client. The helper stays in `client/helpers`, and the TestSetup stays beside the test in `client/tests`.\n\nRun `ty test` in the project folder. Then stop `ty preview` (press Ctrl+C) and run `ty preview` again. It starts, because a test case now calls every public method. A test must really call a method: a call on the fake `greetingClient` does not count for `GreetingClient`, and writing only the name of a method does not count at all. `ty test --coverage` shows which case first called each method.\n\nResult: `ty test` prints one ok line for each test case and ends with 0 failed.",
